.TH IMAGE-SPEED-METER 1 "November 2026" "image-speed-meter 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
image-speed-meter \- measure how fast a file downloads
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B image-speed-meter
[\fIoptions\fR]
\fIurl\fR [\fIurl\fR ...]
.br
.B image-speed-meter
[\fIoptions\fR]
\(aq\fIjson-array-of-urls\fR\(aq
.br
.B image-speed-meter
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B \-\-file
\fIpath\fR
.br
.B image-speed-meter login
\fIurl\fR
[\fB\-\-profile\fR \fIname\fR|\fIdir\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B image-speed-meter
makes
.I N
(10 by default) sequential requests to every given URL.
Each request starts only after the previous response has been received
completely.
For every URL it prints the time and size of each request, then the average
request time, the total amount of data downloaded and the download speed.
.PP
Any file can be measured: images, archives, videos, PDFs, HTML pages, API
responses.
URLs can be passed as separate arguments, as one JSON array, or in a file.
They are measured one after another; a failure on one URL does not stop the
others.
.PP
Requests are made with
.BR curl (1).
In
.B auto
mode, a URL that curl cannot get is retried in headless Chromium
(see
.BR FALLBACK ).
Sites that need authorization are supported through HTTP credentials,
headers, cookies or a saved browser login (see
.BR AUTHENTICATION ).
The last downloaded body is saved to disk unless
.B \-\-no\-save
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-runs " \fIN\fR"
Number of sequential requests per URL. Default: 10.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " \fImode\fR"
How requests are made:
.RS
.TP 9
.B auto
curl first, headless browser if curl is blocked (default).
.TP
.B curl
curl only; a browser is never started.
.TP
.B browser
headless browser only.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIpath\fR"
Read URLs from \fIpath\fR, one per line.
Empty lines are ignored, and \(aq#\(aq starts a comment.
URLs from the file are added after the ones on the command line.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " \fIdir\fR"
Directory to save the downloaded file in. It is created if missing.
Default: \fI./downloads\fR.
The file name comes from the server's Content-Disposition header, then the
last part of the URL path, then the host name; an extension is added from
the Content-Type if the name has none.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-save
Do not save the downloaded file.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fIsec\fR"
Timeout for a single request, in seconds. Default: 60.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the results as a JSON array, one object per URL, instead of text.
No progress lines are printed.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only the per-URL summary, not every request.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SS Authorization options
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " \fIuser\fR[:\fIpassword\fR]"
HTTP Basic or Digest credentials (the scheme is chosen automatically).
Without \fI:password\fR, the password is read from
.B IMAGE_SPEED_METER_PASSWORD
or asked for in the terminal without echo.
A URL of the form \fIhttps://user:password@host/\fR works the same way for
that URL, and the credentials are removed from all output.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-header " \fIheader\fR"
Add a request header such as
\(aqAuthorization: Bearer \fItoken\fR\(aq or \(aqX-Api-Key: \fIkey\fR\(aq.
Repeatable.
\fB@\fIfile\fR reads headers from \fIfile\fR, one per line;
\(aq#\(aq starts a comment.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-cookie " \fIname\fR=\fIvalue\fR[; \fIname\fR=\fIvalue\fR ...]"
Send cookies to the host of each URL. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cookie\-file " \fIpath\fR"
Load cookies from a file in Netscape format, as written by
.BR curl (1)
or by browser cookie-export extensions.
Cookies are sent only to the domains recorded in the file.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " \fIname\fR|\fIdir\fR"
Use a session saved with
.BR "image-speed-meter login" .
A value that contains a slash or starts with \(aq.\(aq or \(aq~\(aq is a
directory; anything else is a profile name (see
.BR FILES ).
.SH LOGIN
.B image-speed-meter login
\fIurl\fR opens a visible Chromium window on \fIurl\fR with a persistent
profile.
Sign in there by any means the site offers (login form, SSO, two-factor
authentication, captcha), then press Enter in the terminal.
The cookies are written to \fIcookies.txt\fR in the profile (also every two
seconds while the window is open), the browser's user agent is remembered,
and the browser is closed.
The profile name defaults to \fBdefault\fR.
.PP
Later runs with \fB\-\-profile\fR reuse the session: curl gets the cookie
file and the user agent, the headless browser gets the whole profile.
Repeat the login when the site's session expires.
A graphical display is required.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Every authorization option applies to both curl and the headless browser.
.PP
Credentials (\fB\-u\fR), headers (\fB\-H\fR) and cookie strings (\fB\-b\fR)
are sent only to the host of the measured URL.
If that host redirects to another host (for example, a file server or a CDN),
they are not sent there.
Domain-scoped cookies from \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-\-profile\fR follow their
own domains.
To guarantee this with curl, redirects are followed one hop at a time when
any authorization option is given; the measured time is the sum of all hops.
.PP
An HTTP 401 response is reported as an authorization error and does not
trigger the browser fallback, because the browser would get the same answer.
.SH OUTPUT
For every request: its number, the method used, its time, its size and its
speed.
For every URL, a summary:
.TP 12
.B method
\fBcurl\fR, \fBbrowser\fR, or \fBcurl + browser\fR if the method changed
during the runs.
.TP
.B requests
number of completed requests.
.TP
.B avg time
mean request time, in seconds (s).
.TP
.B total data
sum of all response bodies, in megabytes (1 MB = 1\|000\|000 bytes), with
the exact byte count in parentheses.
.TP
.B speed
total bytes divided by the total time of all requests, in MB/s and in
Mbit/s (1 Mbit = 1\|000\|000 bits).
.PP
Values below 1 are printed with more decimal places, so small files do not
show up as zero.
.PP
With curl, time and size are taken from curl itself
(\fItime_total\fR and \fIsize_download\fR), so process start-up is not
counted.
With the browser, every successful response is saved as a download; time is
measured from navigation start until the last byte is on disk, and size is
the number of bytes received.
Browser start-up is not counted, and caching is disabled, so every run hits
the network.
.SH FALLBACK
In
.B auto
mode, curl is replaced with headless Chromium for the rest of the runs of a
URL when:
.IP \(bu 2
the response status is 403, 429 or 503;
.IP \(bu
a 2xx response is an anti-bot page (Cloudflare, DDoS-Guard, Imperva,
PerimeterX, DataDome and similar; detected by the \fIcf-mitigated\fR header
and by markers in the HTML).
An ordinary HTML page is measured with curl like any other file;
.IP \(bu
curl itself fails (connection reset, TLS error and so on).
.PP
The request that triggered the fallback is not counted.
If the browser gets a challenge page that runs scripts and then redirects, it
waits for the redirect, then repeats that run so the measured time does not
include the challenge.
Chromium is started once and reused for all URLs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/image-speed-meter/profiles/<name>/
A named login profile (\fI~/.config\fR when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset). Created with mode 0700.
.TP
.I <profile>/cookies.txt
Cookies exported by
.BR login ,
Netscape format, mode 0600.
.TP
.I <profile>/session.json
The user agent of the browser that signed in.
.TP
.I <profile>/chromium/
The Chromium user data directory.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHROME_PATH
Path to a Chromium or Chrome executable.
If unset, the first of
.BR chromium-browser ,
.BR chromium ,
.BR google-chrome-stable ,
.BR google-chrome ,
.BR chrome
and
.B microsoft-edge
found in
.B PATH
is used, then the usual macOS and Windows install locations.
.TP
.B IMAGE_SPEED_METER_PASSWORD
Password for \fB\-\-user\fR when it is not given inline.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for named profiles.
.SH SECURITY
Secrets are never passed to curl on its command line: the credentials,
headers and cookies are written to curl's standard input as a config file
(\fB\-\-config \-\fR).
They are not printed in the output or in the JSON.
.PP
Arguments of \fBimage-speed-meter\fR itself, such as \fB\-u user:password\fR
or \fB\-H \(aqAuthorization: ...\(aq\fR, are visible to other local users in
the process list and are kept in the shell history.
Prefer \fB\-u user\fR with the password prompt or
.BR IMAGE_SPEED_METER_PASSWORD ,
and \fB\-H @file\fR for tokens.
.PP
Cookie files and profiles give access to your accounts: keep them private and
out of version control.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every URL was measured successfully, or the login succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
At least one URL failed, or the login failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments, or a password could not be obtained.
.SH EXAMPLES
Measure one image with the default 10 requests:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Fronalpstock_big.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Three requests to a release archive, browser only:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-n 3 \-\-mode browser \e
  https://github.com/cli/cli/releases/download/v2.63.0/gh_2.63.0_linux_amd64.tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
HTTP Basic or Digest authorization, asking for the password:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-u alice https://example.com/private/report.pdf
.fi
.RE
.PP
A bearer token kept in a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo \(aqAuthorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...\(aq > token.hdr
image-speed-meter \-H @token.hdr https://api.example.com/export.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Cookies exported from a browser:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-c cookies.txt https://example.com/files/big.zip
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sign in once in a real browser window, then measure with the saved session:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter login https://example.com/login
image-speed-meter \-p default https://example.com/files/big.zip
.fi
.RE
.PP
Several URLs as a JSON array, JSON output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-\-json \(aq["https://a.example/1.jpg","https://b.example/2.zip"]\(aq
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR chromium (1)
